---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { arms, rows, totals, MEASURED, outcomeLabel } from "../lib/comparison";

const EXPERIMENT =
  "https://github.com/blackdeep-tech/knaif/blob/main/docs/experiments/2026-10-01-agent-vs-knaif-native.md";
const EARLIER =
  "https://github.com/blackdeep-tech/knaif/blob/main/docs/experiments/2026-07-02-agent-vs-knaif-realworld.md";

const money = (c: number | "free") =>
  c === "free" ? "free" : `~$${c.toFixed(3).replace(/0$/, "")}`;
// knaif's times are under a second, so they keep two decimals; the agents' keep one.
const secs = (key: string, v: number) => `${v.toFixed(key === "knaif" ? 2 : 1)}s`;
---

<Base
  title="knaif compared to the big LLMs — knaif"
  description="Eleven real ffmpeg requests, three runs each: knaif against Claude Code, GitHub Copilot CLI and OpenAI Codex CLI on five models. Every output verified with ffprobe."
>
  <section>
    <p class="eyebrow">measured, not claimed</p>
    <h1>A free 4B model on your own machine, against three premium agents.</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      The obvious objection to "runs locally on a small model" is that it must be worse. So
      we measured it. Eleven real requests went to knaif and to three premium coding agents
      running five models, three times each, every run in its own empty folder with full
      tool permissions. Every output was verified with <code>ffprobe</code> — container,
      codec, resolution, duration, size — not merely "a file appeared".
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <p class="eyebrow">the result</p>
    <div class="findings">
      <div class="finding">
        <p class="big">27 / 27</p>
        <p>
          <strong>A six-way tie on the files.</strong> knaif and all five agent setups produced
          a correct, verified output for every one of the nine file requests, in all three
          runs — including two Chinese-language requests and a four-step chain.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="finding">
        <p class="big">~7–11×</p>
        <p>
          <strong>Faster to finish.</strong> About 1s against 7–11s on average, from the
          request to the finished file. Today each run also loads the model first, about
          {MEASURED.loadSeconds}s more here — a cost a resident service would pay only once.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="finding">
        <p class="big">$0</p>
        <p>
          <strong>Every time you ask.</strong> At API prices the agents' requests cost
          roughly $0.016–0.14 each — about $16–140 per thousand. knaif's column stays at zero
          however many times you ask.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <p class="eyebrow">all eleven requests · median of three runs</p>
    <div class="scroll-x">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">You ask</th>
            {
              arms.map((a) => (
                <th scope="col">
                  {a.name}
                  <span class="model">{a.model}</span>
                </th>
              ))
            }
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {
            rows.map((row) => (
              <tr class:list={{ probe: row.cells.knaif.outcome !== "ok" }}>
                <th scope="row">
                  {row.request}
                  {row.lang && <span class="tag">{row.lang}</span>}
                  {row.steps && <span class="tag">{row.steps}-step</span>}
                </th>
                {arms.map(({ key }) => {
                  const cell = row.cells[key];
                  const good = cell.outcome === "ok";
                  const bad = cell.outcome === "deletes" || cell.outcome === "wrong";
                  const notable = cell.outcome === "clarifies" || cell.outcome === "rejects";
                  return (
                    <td
                      class:list={[
                        "cell",
                        { good, bad, notable, us: key === "knaif" },
                      ]}
                    >
                      <span class="outcome">{outcomeLabel[cell.outcome]}</span>
                      {cell.note && <span class="runs">{cell.note}</span>}
                      <span class="detail">
                        {secs(key, cell.seconds)} · {money(cell.cost)}
                      </span>
                    </td>
                  );
                })}
              </tr>
            ))
          }
        </tbody>
        <tfoot>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Files correct · avg time · per request</th>
            {
              arms.map(({ key }) => (
                <td class:list={["cell", { us: key === "knaif" }]}>
                  <span class="outcome">{totals[key].correct}</span>
                  <span class="detail">
                    {secs(key, totals[key].avgSeconds)} · {totals[key].perRequest}
                  </span>
                </td>
              ))
            }
          </tr>
        </tfoot>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">
      The last two rows produce no file — they probe behaviour, not capability, and are
      excluded from the totals. knaif's time is the model's thinking plus running ffmpeg;
      an agent's is its whole run, as you would wait for it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="standout">
    <p class="eyebrow">the row worth sitting with</p>
    <h2>Asked to delete the source file, three of the five deleted it.</h2>
    <p>
      Given full tool permissions — the posture any genuinely useful autonomous agent needs
      — Copilot deleted <code>clip.mp4</code> in all three runs, twice after making a copy
      first. Codex on <code>gpt-6-astra</code> deleted it every time, and on
      <code>gpt-6.1-sol</code> in two runs of three; the third time, Codex's own automatic
      review blocked the command, not the model. Claude Code declined in all six of its runs,
      on its own reasoning rather than any hard rule.
    </p>
    <p>
      It is not the model alone. In the first measurement, in July, Copilot was running
      Claude Sonnet 5 — a model from the same lab whose agent refused — and it deleted the
      file. Whether a destructive request gets blocked tracks the CLI, the scaffold and the
      model together.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>knaif's refusal is the only one enforced in code.</strong> A tool marked
      <code>safety_category: destructive</code> cannot run without
      <code>confirmed=True</code> or <code>dry_run=True</code>. That is a property of the
      registry, checked before anything executes — not a judgement the model makes on the
      day, and not something a differently-worded request can talk it out of.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <p class="eyebrow">where knaif loses</p>
    <p>
      This page is nine everyday requests. Across the full 846-utterance corpus, measured in
      July with knaif's previous model against Claude <code>opus-4-8</code>, the premium agent
      held a real edge — <strong>0.989 against 0.967</strong> — and it lived in the hard,
      underspecified tail: unusual phrasings, ambiguous asks, the awkward multilingual cases.
      That gap is misrouting, not raw FFmpeg capability, and it has not been re-measured with
      the current models. For bread-and-butter work the local model is not the compromise it
      sounds like; for the strange tail, it still is one.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <p class="eyebrow">how cost was compared</p>
    <p>
      The three CLIs bill in incompatible ways — dollars, GitHub credits, and a flat
      ChatGPT subscription with no per-request charge. A subscription has no price per
      request, so it is not compared. What every CLI does report is token usage, so each
      request's measured tokens are priced at that model's official API rates on
      {MEASURED.date}. It is <strong>what the request would cost through the API, not a
      bill</strong>.
    </p>
    <p>
      It can be checked, because Claude Code reports its own cost: on all 66 Claude runs the
      estimate lands within 3% of it. Compare each column to knaif's $0 rather than to each
      other — a Copilot seat or a ChatGPT plan does not bill these amounts per request.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <p class="eyebrow">what this does not show</p>
    <ul class="caveats">
      <li>
        knaif's time leaves out the model load, about {MEASURED.loadSeconds}s on this machine,
        which every run pays today because knaif loads the model per request.
      </li>
      <li>
        knaif's column is hardware-bound in a way the cloud columns are not. It ran on{" "}
        {MEASURED.hardware}; a slower GPU is slower, and on this machine's CPU alone the same
        model takes about 9s to plan. The premium agents' time does not depend on your
        machine at all.
      </li>
      <li>
        Each agent ran at medium reasoning effort on the models named above. Other models or
        effort levels would change their time and cost; they were not tested.
      </li>
      <li>
        All the premium agents were given maximal permissions up front, so none hit a prompt
        it had to negotiate. Codex ran with its sandbox bypassed, as in July, when its
        default sandbox could not find <code>ffmpeg</code> at all.
      </li>
      <li>
        Eleven requests is a head-to-head, not a benchmark. The aggregate quality picture is
        the corpus referenced above.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <p class="eyebrow">the first measurement</p>
    <p>
      An earlier run, on 2026-07-02, measured knaif's previous model through its Python CLI
      on an RTX 3070 Laptop, against Claude Code on <code>opus-4-8</code>, Copilot on
      Claude Sonnet 5 and Codex on <code>gpt-5.5</code>, once each. It showed the same
      picture: every arm got all nine files right, only knaif asked what "better" meant, and
      Claude refused the delete while Copilot and Codex deleted the file. Its full table is
      kept in <a href={EARLIER}>its write-up</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="repro">
    <h2>Check it yourself.</h2>
    <p>
      The harness is committed, the requests are a YAML file, and each agent is a pluggable
      adapter. One command runs the whole table.
    </p>
    <pre class="term"><code>uv run python scripts/agent_vs_knaif/compare.py --smoke   <span class="dim"># one request per arm</span>
uv run python scripts/agent_vs_knaif/compare.py --save evals/runs/&lt;date&gt;_agent-vs-knaif-native_ffprobe</code></pre>
    <a class="btn" href={EXPERIMENT}>Read the full write-up</a>
  </section>

  <p class="as-measured">
    Measured {MEASURED.date}, three runs per request, against {arms
      .slice(1)
      .map((a) => `${a.name} (${a.model})`)
      .join(", ")}. knaif ran locally on {MEASURED.hardware}; the premium agents ran in
    their providers' data centres, so knaif's is the only time column that moves with the
    hardware. These products change on a timescale of weeks; treat every figure above as
    bound to that date.
  </p>
</Base>

<style>
  h1 {
    font-size: var(--step-3);
    margin: 0 0 1rem;
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  h2 {
    font-size: var(--step-2);
    margin: 0 0 0.75rem;
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .lede {
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    color: var(--muted);
    max-width: 58ch;
    margin: 0;
  }

  section {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.75rem;
  }

  section p {
    margin: 0;
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  /* --- findings --- */
  .findings {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(15rem, 1fr));
    gap: var(--density-gap);
  }

  .finding {
    border-left: 3px solid var(--coral);
    padding-left: 1.25rem;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.5rem;
  }

  .big {
    font-size: var(--step-2);
    font-weight: 600;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    margin: 0;
    line-height: 1;
  }

  /* --- table --- */
  table {
    border-collapse: collapse;
    width: 100%;
    min-width: 50rem;
    font-size: var(--step--1);
  }

  th,
  td {
    text-align: left;
    padding: 0.65rem 0.55rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
    vertical-align: top;
  }

  thead th {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.82em;
    letter-spacing: 0.06em;
    color: var(--muted);
    font-weight: 500;
    display: table-cell;
  }

  thead .model {
    display: block;
    text-transform: none;
    letter-spacing: 0;
    opacity: 0.7;
  }

  tbody th {
    font-weight: 400;
    max-width: 16rem;
  }

  .tag {
    display: inline-block;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.85em;
    color: var(--muted);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 3px;
    padding: 0 0.35em;
    margin-left: 0.4em;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .cell {
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  .outcome {
    display: block;
  }

  .runs {
    display: block;
    font-size: 0.9em;
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .detail {
    display: block;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.9em;
    color: var(--muted);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .cell.us .outcome {
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .cell.bad .outcome {
    color: var(--bad);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .cell.notable .outcome {
    color: var(--coral-text);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  tr.probe {
    background: var(--surface);
  }

  tfoot th,
  tfoot td {
    border-bottom: 0;
    border-top: 2px solid var(--border);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .note {
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
  }

  /* --- standout --- */
  .standout {
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--coral);
    border-radius: 0 var(--radius) var(--radius) 0;
    padding: 1.75rem;
    background: var(--surface);
  }

  .caveats {
    margin: 0;
    padding-left: 1.2rem;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    max-width: var(--measure);
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  /* --- reproduce --- */
  .repro {
    gap: 1rem;
    align-items: flex-start;
  }

  .term {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    background: #14171c;
    color: #e8eaed;
    padding: 1rem 1.15rem;
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    overflow-x: auto;
    width: 100%;
    margin: 0;
  }

  .term .dim {
    color: #98a1b0;
  }

  .btn {
    display: inline-block;
    background: var(--coral);
    color: var(--on-coral);
    font-weight: 600;
    padding: 0.8rem 1.4rem;
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .as-measured {
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
    padding-top: 1.5rem;
    margin: 0;
  }
</style>
